Allow builds to opt out of stdexec's automatic CUDA atomic selection.

stdexec uses cuda::std::atomic instead of std::atomic whenever
<cuda/std/atomic> can be found, even if nothing is compiled for CUDA.

Without CUDA (HIP builds included), an old libcu++ in a default include
directory (e.g. from the CUDA packages of Ubuntu 22.04) then breaks the build,
as it can't be compiled without nvcc and has no cuda::std::atomic_ref
(HPX #7884).

With SYCL, CUDA 12.9 and Intel LLVM v6.0.1 declare __assert_fail with
incompatible exception specifications. When CUDA headers are visible to a
SYCL compiler, including cuda/std/atomic can report "exception specification
in declaration does not match previous declaration" for __assert_fail.

HPX exports STDEXEC_NO_CUDA_STD_ATOMIC to all translation units and consumers
of builds without CUDA and of SYCL builds so that they use the same atomic
types. CUDA builds without SYCL retain stdexec's original selection. A failed
patch is fatal only when SYCL is enabled; an unrelated stdexec revision must
not break non-SYCL builds.

This applies to stdexec 04de75de5807e73a229acf047c6976575e313359.
NVIDIA/stdexec#2042 discusses automatic CUDA atomic selection; it does not
track the SYCL declaration conflict. Retest that conflict before dropping
this workaround when updating the pin.

